#X obj 60 40 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty juno.i~\ and\ juno~ 24 6 0 22 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 76 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Roland\ Juno-60\ with\ Juno-106\ patches\ and\ SysEx 24 6 0 16 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 120 juno.i~ 1;
#X text 190 120 <- click it: the editor, f 64;
#X obj 60 160 dac~;
#X obj 60 206 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty juno.i~ 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 234 juno.i~ is the whole instrument in one object: juno~ \, the editor \, the controller layer \, the Launchkey pads and display \, MIDI in and out. Put it in a patch \, connect the two left outlets to dac~ and play. Click it to open the editor \, see Editor. \; Argument 1 is the MIDI channel (0 or none = 1) \, argument 2 the channel of the pads (0 or none = 10 \, the Drums Channel). Several juno.i~ do not get in each other's way \, each has its own editor. \; juno~ is the bare engine inside \, for patches of your own: inlet 2 takes the velocity of a note sent as a float \, inlet 3 raw MIDI bytes from midiin \, and the floats of outlet 3 (SysEx \, MIDI thru) go to midiout. Open juno.i~ to see the wiring., f 64;
#X obj 60 456 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Editor 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 484 The editor is the window of juno.i~. Every change shows up in it \, from the controller \, a patch change \, messages or SysEx. refresh asks for all values again. \; Rows of 8 below the PATCH band \, titled as in Controls: row k is page k of the controller layer. Each cell: name and label \, a slider and a number box with the value as from a pot (0 to 127) \, the box beside it the value as on the panel. Edits are heard at once. The tinted rows (SETUP \, VOICES) belong to the instrument and are not stored in a patch. \; PATCH band. name: the playing patch. factory N \, user N: choose a factory preset or a user slot. reload: the same patch again \, edits dropped. init patch: a plain sawtooth. store to N: chooses the user slot \, save patch stores the playing patch there and writes the user bank \, see Presets. load bank \, write bank: read or write the bank file without storing. send bank \, send patch: as SysEx to MIDI out \, see SysEx. \; MIDI in: the receive channel \, 0 = all. out: the channel of the controller feedback and of SysEx. SysEx for every change: see SysEx. MIDI thru: see Messages \, thru., f 64;
#X obj 60 802 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Launchkey 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 830 Launchkey: the Novation Launchkey Mini MK4 with the custom firmware maltepeter2000/launchkey_firmware \, set up the same for every instrument of bela-workstation (launchkey/README.md there). \; junocc 1: the controller layer. Encoders in mode Custom 1 send CC pages from 20 in rows of 8 on the keys channel \, every change made elsewhere goes back to them on the send channel (txchannel of juno~) \, CC 3 asks for all values. \; junopads 10: the pads in Drum mode (notes 36-51 on channel 10 \, the Drums Channel): top row 36 factory + \, 37 user + \, 38 init (press twice within a second) \, 39-43 own \, bottom row 44 factory - \, 45 user - \, 46 save \, 47-51 own. Own pads of juno~: 39 chorus I \, II \, I+II \, off (round and round) \, 47 Juno-106 / Juno-60. \; Storing asks first: save once shows Store to? and the user slot played or stored last (savearm) \, user + / - choose the slot (saveslot 1 / -1 \, nothing is recalled) \, save again stores there and writes the user bank (save). Any other pad or 10 s cancel (savecancel). Own pads and init after its first press show what they do on the display. \; Pad colours \, sent by junopads itself with noteout on channel 10 (velocity = colour + 32 x mode): factory blue \, user and save green (the user bank) \, init orange (flashing while the second press is awaited) \, save flashing while it asks and white for a moment when stored \, own pads purple \, free pads off. They go out at load \, on leds \, and when the Launchkey asks for them (CC 3 = 127 on channel 10 \, sent when Drum mode is selected). \; lkdisplay: the display lines (display <title> <value>: the control you turn \, Preset \, User \, Init or Stored with the patch name) as SysEx for the Launchkey's display -> midiout., f 64;
#X obj 60 1340 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Inlet 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1368 1 float: note number \, velocity 100. \; 1 list: note \, velocity and \, if you like \, the MIDI channel. \; 1 anything: a message \, see Messages., f 64;
#X obj 60 1430 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Outlets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1458 1 signal: left output. \; 2 signal: right output. \; 3 anything: info \, name \, 0-127 and value text for every \;   change \, the patch that plays \, its name \, the channels. \; 3 display title value: two lines for a display: a control \;   (VCF freq \, 64) \, the patch that plays (Preset 12 \, \;   Harpsichord 1) \, a store (Stored \, User 11)., f 64;
#X obj 60 1584 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Presets\ and\ user\ bank 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1612 preset selects a factory preset \, user a slot of the user bank. The third outlet answers with the number and the name. \; The user bank (56 slots \, shown as bank and number 11 to 78) is junouser-bank.syx in the folder of your patch \, so every project keeps its own. juno.i~ reads it at load if it is there \, otherwise the bank beside juno~ is the starting point. save \, userwrite and userread without a file name use this file: store to N in the editor chooses the slot \, save patch stores and writes. Save the patch first \, an unsaved patch has no folder of its own. \; There is one user bank in Pd \, not one per object: two juno.i~ in one patch share it. \; The controls of the SETUP row \, volume to velcutoff \, belong to the instrument. A patch change leaves them alone and they are not stored., f 64;
#X obj 60 1866 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Messages 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1894 name v: set a control \, in the values the panel shows. \; name: report it as info. \; n name v: set it from a pot or a MIDI CC \, 0 to 127. \; ninfo name: report it as info. \; preset n: a factory preset \, read only. \; user n: a slot of the user bank \, 0 to 55. \; init: a plain sawtooth patch to start from. \; reload: the same patch again \, edits dropped. \; store n: the playing patch into user slot n \, in memory. \;   Without n: the user slot chosen or stored last. \; save: store \, then write the user bank (the pads' save). \; savearm \, saveslot n \, savecancel: the pads' store with a \;   question: show the slot \, move it \, drop it. save after \;   savearm stores into the slot shown. \; presetstep 1 or -1 \, userstep 1 or -1: the next or previous \;   factory preset or user slot \, from the one chosen last. \; cycle name [-1]: the next (previous) value \, round and round. \; userwrite [file] \, userread [file]: the user bank \, see \;   Presets. A file name without a path is beside juno~. \; usersend: the user bank as SysEx. \; presets \, users: list the names. \; bend f: pitch wheel \, -1 to 1 \, times bend. \; mod f: modulation wheel \, 0 to 1: vibrato. \; sustain 0/1: hold pedal. \; channel n: MIDI receive channel \, 0 is all. \; txchannel n: channel of the controller feedback and of \;   SysEx \, 1 to 16. \; thru 0/1: MIDI thru \, on by default: what arrives at MIDI in \;   leaves MIDI out unchanged. Off with several juno.i~ or \;   when MIDI out leads back to MIDI in. \; sysexout 0/1: SysEx for every change \, see SysEx. \; param a v: one of the 18 patch bytes \, see SysEx. \;   param a reports it. \; dump: every control as info. \; sysexdump: the playing patch as SysEx. \; status: report patch \, channels and sounding voices. \; stop: all notes off. \; reset: all notes off at once \, chorus emptied., f 64;
#X obj 60 2516 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty The\ instrument 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2544 The engine is a Juno-60: one DCO per voice with sawtooth \, pulse \, sub oscillator and noise \, the four-position high-pass \, the IR3109 low-pass \, one envelope \, one LFO and the chorus with its two bucket-brigade lines. Six voices \, handed out in turn as on the instrument. \; A patch is kept in the format of the Juno-106: 16 sliders from 0 to 127 and 8 switches. So Juno-106 patch collections play \, and patches go to a Juno-106 and back. Chorus I+II of the Juno-60 is kept (a Juno-106 plays it as I) \, pulse width from the envelope and the arpeggiator are left out. \; Not on the instrument: velocity \, off until vellevel or velcutoff is raised \, and the modulation wheel \, which adds vibrato as on a Juno-106. model switches between the behaviour of the Juno-60 and the Juno-106. The oscillator and the filter run at twice the sample rate., f 64;
#X obj 60 2798 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty SysEx 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2826 The object speaks the exclusive format of the Juno-106 on MIDI in and out \, on the receive channel. A patch message (F0 41 30) or a manual message (31) replaces the playing patch \, a parameter message (F0 41 32 channel parameter value F7) changes one of its 18 bytes. sysexout 1 sends every edit and patch change the same way \, like a Juno-106. send patch and send bank in the editor send without being asked. A Juno-106 plays what it receives but stores it only from its own panel. \; The 18 bytes: 0 to 15 are the sliders \, LFO rate \, LFO delay \, DCO LFO \, PWM \, noise \, VCF frequency \, resonance \, envelope \, LFO \, keyboard \, VCA level \, attack \, decay \, sustain \, release \, sub level. 16: bits 0 to 2 16' \, 8' \, 4' \, bit 3 pulse \, bit 4 sawtooth \, bit 5 chorus off \, bit 6 chorus I \, else II. 17: bit 0 PWM manual \, bit 1 envelope negative \, bit 2 VCA gate \, bits 3 and 4 the high-pass (3 for position 0 down to 0 for position 3) \, bit 5 chorus I+II (the object's own)., f 64;
#X obj 60 3096 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Data\ files 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3124 juno~ needs no files: the 56 factory sounds of the Juno-60 are built in \, presets 0 to 55. Every .syx file in the folder of juno~ is read as a bank of Juno-106 patches and joins the factory presets in alphabetical order \, from preset 56 on \, named after the file and the place a Juno-106 shows (A11 to B88). \; For juno.i~ two files are enough: juno~ (the external) and juno.i~.pd in a folder on Pd's search path., f 64;
#X obj 60 3266 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Controls 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3294 In the order of the editor: one heading per row \, the same title as the row there \, page k on the controller is row k. Each line: name (the label in the editor) \, range \, CC number and what it does., f 64;
#X obj 60 3372 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 1\ \ PERFORM\ \ CC\ 20-27 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3400 cutoff (VCF freq) 0..127 \, CC 20: cutoff of the low-pass filter \; res (VCF res) 0..127 \, CC 21: resonance \, up to self-oscillation \; attack (Attack) 0..127 \, CC 22: envelope attack \; release (Release) 0..127 \, CC 23: envelope release \; dcolfo (DCO LFO) 0..127 \, CC 24: LFO to pitch (vibrato) \; chorus (Chorus) 0..3 \, CC 25: chorus. I+II is the Juno-60's \;   third mode \, a Juno-106 plays it as I \; env (VCF env) 0..127 \, CC 26: envelope to cutoff \; volume (Volume) 0..127 \, CC 27: master volume \, after the chorus, f 64;
#X obj 60 3558 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 2\ \ DCO\ \ CC\ 28\ 29\ 30\ 31\ 33\ 34\ 35\ 36 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3586 range (DCO range) 0..2 \, CC 28: octave of the oscillator \; pulse (Pulse) 0..1 \, CC 29: pulse wave \; saw (Sawtooth) 0..1 \, CC 30: sawtooth wave \; sub (Sub level) 0..127 \, CC 31: level of the sub oscillator \, \;   one octave down \; noise (Noise) 0..127 \, CC 33: noise level \; pwm (PWM) 0..127 \, CC 34: pulse width: the depth of the LFO \, or \;   the width itself with pwmmode MAN \; pwmmode (PWM mode) 0..1 \, CC 35: pulse width from the LFO or \;   fixed \; hpf (HPF) 0..3 \, CC 36: high-pass filter \, four positions, f 64;
#X obj 60 3776 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 3\ \ ENV\ /\ LFO\ /\ VCF\ /\ VCA\ \ CC\ 37\ 39\ 40\ 41\ 42\ 43\ 44\ 45 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3804 decay (Decay) 0..127 \, CC 37: envelope decay \; sustain (Sustain) 0..127 \, CC 39: envelope sustain level \; lforate (LFO rate) 0..127 \, CC 40: LFO rate \, 0.3 to 20 Hz \; lfodelay (LFO delay) 0..127 \, CC 41: time until the LFO sets in \;   after the first key of a phrase \; vcflfo (VCF LFO) 0..127 \, CC 42: LFO to cutoff \; kybd (VCF kybd) 0..127 \, CC 43: keyboard follow of the cutoff \; envpol (VCF env pol) 0..1 \, CC 44: polarity of the envelope on \;   the cutoff \; level (VCA level) 0..127 \, CC 45: level of the patch \, in front \;   of the chorus, f 64;
#X obj 60 3994 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 4\ \ VCA\ \ CC\ 46-46 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4022 vcamode (VCA mode) 0..1 \, CC 46: the amplifier follows the \;   envelope or the gate, f 64;
#X obj 60 4068 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 5\ \ SETUP\ \ CC\ 54-59 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4096 tune (Tune) -50..50 \, CC 54: master tune in cents \; bend (Bend range) 0..7 \, CC 55: bender range in semitones \; octave (Octave) -2..2 \, CC 56: octave transposition of the \;   keyboard \; vellevel (Velo level) 0..127 \, CC 57: velocity to level. 0 is \;   the instrument \, which has no velocity \; velcutoff (Velo cutoff) 0..127 \, CC 58: velocity to cutoff \, a \;   soft key is darker \; model (Model) 0..1 \, CC 59: which Juno the patch plays on: the \;   Juno-60 \, or the Juno-106 as measured on Cherry Audio's \;   DCO-106 (cutoff and resonance \, contour times and depth \, LFO \, \;   high-pass \, chorus \, sub and noise levels \, pulse width \, VCA \;   level), f 64;
#X obj 60 4318 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 6\ \ VOICES\ \ CC\ 62-63 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4346 calibrate (Calibrate) 0..127 \, CC 62: how far the six voices \;   drift apart \, as on an instrument out of calibration: each \;   voice its own tuning \, cutoff \, resonance and level. 0 is six \;   identical voices \; chorusnoise (Chorus hiss) 0..127 \, CC 63: hiss of the chorus \;   lines \, heard while the chorus is on. 0 is none, f 64;
#X text 82 4462 Credits: Modelled on PicoFaceJ6 from PicoVintageSynthCollection by Michi71\; Pure Data adaptation with help by Claude, f 64;
#X connect 5 0 7 0;
#X connect 5 1 7 1;
